This confirms it: the `internal/scheduler` package is never actually instantiated or used anywhere in the server startup code — it exists only as a standalone cron-scheduling library with its own tests/docs.

r03. There is no nightly database-cleanup job actually configured in this repository's scheduler. The premise of the question is incorrect: `internal/scheduler` (`/bench/repo/internal/scheduler/`) is a self-contained, generic cron-scheduling library (see `/bench/repo/internal/scheduler/scheduler.go`, `job.go`, `parser.go`) that ships with example/documentation code only — it is never instantiated or wired up anywhere else in the codebase (no `scheduler.New(...)` or `Register(...)` call exists outside the package itself, e.g. in `server/`, `cmd/`, or `store/`).

The "2 AM daily" example that mentions a `"daily-cleanup"` job is purely illustrative documentation in `/bench/repo/internal/scheduler/README.md` (lines 30-48), not a real, registered job:

```30:48:internal/scheduler/README.md
func main() {
    s := scheduler.New()

    s.Register(&scheduler.Job{
        Name:     "daily-cleanup",
        Schedule: "0 2 * * *", // 2 AM daily
        Handler: func(ctx context.Context) error {
            fmt.Println("Running cleanup...")
            return nil
        },
    })

    s.Start()
    defer s.Stop(context.Background())

    // Keep running...
    select {}
}
```

Similarly, `example_test.go` in the same package contains only generic sample jobs (e.g., `"stale-session-cleanup"` at `"0 3 * * *"` in the Best Practices section), not a job actually named "database-cleanup."

The only real, running cleanup logic I found in the app is unrelated in-memory cache eviction on a 5-minute ticker (not a cron schedule, and not database cleanup) in `/bench/repo/store/cache/cache.go` (`CleanupInterval: 5 * time.Minute`, `cleanupLoop()`) and `/bench/repo/store/store.go` (line 42).

So there is no nightly "database-cleanup" cron job configured anywhere in this repo that I could find.
